.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-cartridge-retire 1  "tapectl-cartridge-retire " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-cartridge\-retire \- Retire a cartridge permanently — it must never be written again
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl cartridge retire\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-reason\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fICARTRIDGE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Retire a cartridge permanently — it must never be written again
.PP
ADR\-0011: for wear, read errors, or any judgement that the medium is no longer fit to hold data. This is NOT an erasure and NOT a location change: the bytes may still be readable, but nothing will ever write to this cartridge again, and `volume init` refuses to bind it. `cartridge unretire` is the way back — the operator saying they were wrong about the medium; `cartridge mark\-erased` is a different statement, that the bytes are gone (ADR\-0011, corrected 2026\-09\-14).
.PP
ADR\-0008 Tier 2: the coverage impact is displayed first, and consent is asked EVERY time — retiring a medium permanently is a declaration worth confirming even when no unit loses coverage by it. `\-\-force`/`\-\-yes` supplies that consent.
.PP
This said consent was "required when a unit is left below its policy" until 2026\-09\-17, which read as "not required otherwise" — so a script retiring a fully\-covered cartridge without `\-\-yes` was refused by a non\-interactive session for a reason the help denied.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = done; 2 = an error or a refusal; 75 = the catalog was busy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fI<REASON>\fR
Why (appended to the cartridge\*(Aqs notes, never overwriting them)
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Give the Tier\-2 consent this command asks on every run; never the Tier\-3 refusal.

Supply the ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 consent this command asks on every run, including when no unit is left below its policy. It does NOT defeat the Tier\-3 refusal — a cartridge holding the last eligible copy of a live version is refused outright and no flag reaches it.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fICARTRIDGE\fR>
The cartridge: its barcode, or the serial its chip reports
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
